@node Overview
@chapter Overview

@samp{color-extras.el} extends Emacs' built-in @code{color} library with
practical tools for working with color values in everyday editing.
Theme authors@comma{} CSS developers@comma{} and anyone who manipulates color codes
in source files will find it useful.

The package provides three main capabilities:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Conversion} between hexadecimal (HEX) and Hue-Saturation-Luminance
(HSL) color representations@comma{} either for the color at point or for
every color in a buffer (@ref{Conversion, , Conversion commands}).
@item
@strong{Contrast analysis} against white@comma{} black@comma{} and an optional reference
color@comma{} following the WCAG contrast ratio standard
(@ref{Contrast analysis, , Contrast analysis}).
@item
@strong{Embark integration} that lets you act on any HEX or HSL color
string with a single keystroke (@ref{Embark integration, , Embark integration}).
@end itemize

Internally@comma{} the package relies on Emacs' built-in @code{color-rgb-to-hsl}
and @code{color-hsl-to-rgb} for the mathematical conversions and on the
@samp{ct} package for contrast ratio computation.

@node Variables
@chapter Variables

@menu
* Regular expression patterns::
@end menu

@node Regular expression patterns
@section Regular expression patterns

@vindex color-extras-hex-pattern
The constant @code{color-extras-hex-pattern} is a regular expression that
matches a six-digit hexadecimal RGB color string@comma{} with or without a
leading @samp{#}.  The first capture group contains the six hex digits.
This pattern is used throughout the package to detect HEX colors at
point and in buffer-wide searches (@ref{Conversion, , Conversion commands}).

@vindex color-extras-hsl-scaled-pattern
The constant @code{color-extras-hsl-scaled-pattern} is a regular expression
that matches an HSL color written as three comma-separated numbers
(e.g.@comma{} @samp{210@comma{} 50%@comma{} 40%}).  Hue ranges from 0 to 360; saturation and
luminance range from 0 to 100 and may include trailing @samp{%}  signs.
The three capture groups correspond to hue@comma{} saturation@comma{} and luminance.

