@node Conversion
@section Conversion

@findex color-extras-convert
When you need to switch a color between HEX and HSL notation@comma{} place
point on the color value and call @code{color-extras-convert}.  If the
color at point is HEX (e.g.@comma{} @samp{#ff0000})@comma{} the command converts it to
HSL (e.g.@comma{} @samp{0@comma{} 100%@comma{} 50%}); if it is HSL@comma{} the command produces HEX@.
The converted value is saved to the kill ring and reported in the echo
area@comma{} so you can immediately yank it wherever you need it.  If no
recognizable color is found at point@comma{} the command reports this in the
echo area.  You can also pass a COLOR argument from Lisp code.

@findex color-extras-convert-all
To convert every color in a file at once@comma{} use
@code{color-extras-convert-all}.  It prompts for the target format---either
@samp{"hex"} or @samp{"hsl"}---and replaces all colors matching the @emph{opposite}
format throughout the buffer.  For example@comma{} choosing @samp{"hex"} finds all
HSL strings and rewrites them as HEX values.  An optional FILE
argument lets you operate on a file other than the current buffer.

@node Contrast analysis
@section Contrast analysis

@findex color-extras-contrast
Verifying that foreground and background colors meet accessibility
guidelines is a frequent need.  The command @code{color-extras-contrast}
computes the WCAG contrast ratio between a primary color and both
white (@samp{#ffffff}) and black (@samp{#000000})@comma{} displaying the results in the
echo area.  If you supply an optional second color@comma{} the command also
reports the contrast ratio between the two.

The primary color defaults to the color at point; if none is found@comma{}
the command prompts for one.  The second color is always prompted for
and may be left blank.  This command requires the @samp{ct} package for its
@code{ct-contrast-ratio} function.

